        "title": "Exactly-Once Stream Processing — Foundations & Mathematical Invariants",
        "say": [
          "In distributed messaging architectures, network packet losses, broker timeouts, and consumer process restarts create duplicate deliveries unless stream semantics enforce strict end-to-end exactly-once processing (EOS).",
          "Engineers distinguish three distinct delivery semantic tiers: at-most-once (messages drop silently during failures, zero duplicates, data loss risk), at-least-once (retries prevent message drops but introduce duplicate records during network timeouts), and effectively-once (stream transformations produce the exact side-effects that would occur if each record was processed once without duplication).",
          "True physical exactly-once delivery over an unreliable network is fundamentally impossible due to the Two Generals problem: an acknowledgment packet can always be delayed or lost after a receiver commits state.",
          "Consequently, enterprise streaming platforms achieve effectively-once processing through two complementary mechanisms: idempotent producer sequence numbers at the ingress broker boundary, and atomic two-phase transactional checkpointing (such as Apache Flink's Chandy-Lamport variant or Kafka's transactional producer coordinator) across downstream state sinks.",
          "Every record must carry a monotonically increasing producer epoch and sequence number `(producer_id, epoch, sequence_id)` so destination partition logs discard duplicate network retries deterministically.",
          "Inspect the formal streaming delivery invariant model below comparing dropped messages, duplicate replays, and idempotent deduplication.",
          "In production message brokers such as Kafka or Pulsar, network failures between the client producer and the broker partition leader frequently occur after the broker has written the message to disk but before transmitting the TCP ACK. The producer's retry mechanism sends the payload again, which creates silent duplicate records in downstream analytics unless sequence numbers filter them out.",
          "By binding the producer session epoch to every batch write, brokers verify monotonically increasing sequence offsets per partition, ensuring that replayed batches are recognized as duplicate receipts and acknowledged without creating secondary log entries."
        ],
        "example": "A bank payment stream deduplicating transaction IDs so customer mobile retries during tunnel disconnects charge checking balances exactly once.",
        "code": "class StreamDeliveryModel:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.log = []\n        self.seen_seqs = {}\n\n    def produce_at_least_once(self, pid, seq, payload):\n        # Naive append on every network retry duplicates records!\n        self.log.append((pid, seq, payload))\n\n    def produce_idempotent(self, pid, seq, payload):\n        last_seq = self.seen_seqs.get(pid, -1)\n        if seq == last_seq + 1:\n            self.log.append((pid, seq, payload))\n            self.seen_seqs[pid] = seq\n            return \"COMMITTED\"\n        elif seq <= last_seq:\n            return \"DUPLICATE_IGNORED\"\n        return \"OUT_OF_ORDER\"\n\nm = StreamDeliveryModel()\nm.produce_idempotent(\"p1\", 0, \"login\")\nm.produce_idempotent(\"p1\", 0, \"login\")  # client retry!\nprint(\"idempotent log len:\", len(m.log), \"status:\", m.produce_idempotent(\"p1\", 1, \"checkout\"))",
